NAHPU exports project data, media, statistics, backups, and settings in
formats tailored to each task. Project exports start from the project
`Dashboard` menu; `Backup database`, `Export user configs`, and
`Import user configs` are also available from the home screen menu.

## Which export do I need?

| You want to… | Use | You get |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Move a project to another device, or keep a full copy | `Export project` {% ref id="B" /%} | A project archive that NAHPU can import or merge |
| Combine a teammate's project file with yours | `Merge project` {% ref id="A" /%} | Their records merged into the open project |
| Send records to a database, spreadsheet, or script | [Export records](/en/usages/export/export-records/) {% ref id="D" /%} | A CSV, TSV, Excel, or JSON table |
| Publish or archive data with its files | [Bundle records](/en/usages/export/export-bundles/) {% ref id="C" /%} | A Darwin Core bundle or NAHPU Data Package |
| Print labels, tags, catalogs, or field books | [Export documents](/en/usages/export/export-documents/) {% ref id="E" /%} | A PDF |
| Report counts, such as specimens per species | [Export statistics](/en/usages/export/export-statistics/) | A summary table as CSV, TSV, Excel, or JSON |
| Pull out photographs and other media files | [Export images](/en/usages/export/export-images/) | One file, or a TAR.GZ or ZIP archive of many |
| Protect everything on this device | [`Backup database`](#backup-database) {% ref id="F" /%} | One archive of the whole installation |
| Share your settings, vocabularies, and presets | [`Export user configs`](#export-user-configs) {% ref id="G" /%} | A JSON or JSON.GZ config file |

Only three of these can bring data back into NAHPU:

- a **project export**, via `Import project` on the home screen or
  `Merge project` in an open project
  ([Project transfer](/en/usages/export/project-transfer/));
- a **NAHPU Data Package**, which carries the same project transfer payload;
- a **database backup**, which replaces the whole installation
  ([Restoring a database](#restoring-a-database)).

Record tables, Darwin Core bundles, documents, and statistics are one-way:
NAHPU cannot rebuild a project from them. Importing a project-info JSON or QR
code creates only the project's name and metadata, without records or media.

## Backup strategy {% #backup-strategy %}

Keep a verified copy of each field day's work off the device you collect on.
Combine two kinds of backup:

| Backup | How often | Holds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Project export | Every field day | One project, with its media in TAR.GZ or ZIP |
| Database backup | Weekly, and before importing, merging, or replacing data | Every project, file, and setting on the device |

{% steps %}

1. Open the project `Dashboard` menu and select `Export project`.
2. Check the record counts and any missing-file warnings. Leave `Light export`
   off and choose `TAR.GZ` or `ZIP` so media are included; `Light export`
   (`JSON.GZ`) holds records only and is not a full backup.
3. Save the archive with the project name and date (or turn on `Append date`),
   then copy it to separate storage or another device.
4. Open the copy to verify it is readable, and periodically test-import it on
   another installation.
5. At your regular checkpoint, create a `Backup database` {% ref id="F" /%}.
   Keep the previous checked backup until the new one is verified.

{% /steps %}

To recover a single project, use `Import project` on the home screen or
`Merge project` inside an existing project.

## Backup database {% #backup-database %}

`Backup database` saves the entire installation in one `TAR.GZ` (default) or
`ZIP` archive {% ref id="H" /%}: the SQLite database plus the project,
personnel, and settings folders, including files no current record links to. A
bare `.sqlite3` file holds records only, without media or other files.

Open `Backup database` from the home screen menu, the project `Dashboard` menu,
or `Settings` > `Database`.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Backup database` {% ref id="F" /%}.
2. Choose `TAR.GZ` or `ZIP` {% ref id="H" /%}, enter a `File name` (optionally
   turn on `Append date`), and choose a destination folder on desktop.
3. Review the backup summary and select `Save backup` {% ref id="I" /%}. You
   can cancel while the archive is being written.
4. Copy the archive off the device and verify it before removing an older
   backup.

{% /steps %}

## Export user configs {% #export-user-configs %}

`Export user configs` {% ref id="G" /%} saves your configuration so another
device—or a future installation—uses the same setup without touching project
data. You can open `Export user configs` from the home screen menu, the project
`Dashboard` menu, or `Setup NAHPU`.

Choose which sections to export:

- `Controlled vocabularies and settings`
- `Tabular export presets`
- `Document templates`
- `Document layout presets`
- `Template table preview columns`
- `Custom fields` (including selecting specific custom field definitions)

{% steps %}

1. Open `Export user configs` from the home screen menu, the project
   `Dashboard` menu, or `Setup NAHPU`.
2. Select the configuration sections to include. If `Custom fields` is checked,
   choose which definitions to export.
3. Review what will be written under `Will export` (under the `Preview` tab on
   phones).
4. Choose `JSON (.json)` or the smaller `JSON.GZ (.json.gz)`, set the
   `File name` (with optional `Append date`), and choose a destination folder
   on desktop.
5. Select `Export user configs`. Use `Share`, open the folder, or save a copy
   once the file is written.

{% /steps %}

## Restoring a database {% #restoring-a-database %}

Restoring a database replaces everything in NAHPU on this device with a backup.

{% aside type="caution" %}

Keep `Back up current data first` turned on unless you are sure you no longer
need the data on this device. A database restore cannot be undone.

{% /aside %}

{% steps %}

1. Open `Settings`.
2. Under `Database`, select `Replace database`.
3. Under `Replace with`, select `Browse` and choose a `.sqlite3` file, a
   `ZIP` archive, or a `TAR.GZ` archive.
4. Compare the two databases. NAHPU shows schema version, records, files, and
   size side by side. Older backups upgrade automatically after restore; files
   from a newer NAHPU version cannot be restored until you update NAHPU.
5. Keep `Back up current data first` turned on so NAHPU saves your current data
   as a `TAR.GZ` archive in its backup folder.
6. Select `Replace database` and confirm the warning.

{% /steps %}

What a restore does:

- Replaces the active database with the one in the backup.
- From an archive, copies project files into NAHPU's documents folder and
  imports settings from the archive. Personnel photos from older backups move
  to the personnel photo folder.
- Overwrites a media or settings file with the same name as one in the archive;
  identical files are left alone.
- Keeps files that exist only on this device, so they appear in later backups.

## Restoring user configs {% #restoring-user-configs %}

Import a user-config file to apply controlled vocabularies, presets, templates,
layouts, or custom fields from another device or backup. Like exporting,
`Import user configs` is available from the home screen menu, the project
`Dashboard` menu, and `Setup NAHPU`.

{% steps %}

1. Open `Import user configs` from the home screen menu, the project
   `Dashboard` menu, or `Setup NAHPU`.
2. Choose a `.json` or `.json.gz` user-config file.
3. Select the sections to import and review them under `Will import` (under the
   `Preview` tab on phones). If `Custom fields` is checked and a project is
   open, set `Custom field destination` to `Global` or `Current project`.
4. Select `Import selected configs` and confirm with `Import`.

{% /steps %}

Selected sections replace the matching settings on this device, except
`Custom fields`, which match by template ID and merge so existing definitions
are kept. Unselected sections stay unchanged. If imported document templates
reference fonts not installed on this device, NAHPU prompts you to pick
replacements.

This changes settings only, not project records. To restore both together,
restore a `ZIP` or `TAR.GZ` database backup. For technical storage details, see
[Data import and export](../../contributing/code/data-import-export-overview).

## Archiving a project {% #archiving-a-project %}

An archive keeps a finished project usable years later by people who did not
collect it. Store the data together with enough context to identify what it is,
when it was made, which software created it, and who curates it.

Before archiving:

- Finish curating the data and fix or document known issues. See
  [Data Management](../../best-practices/data-management).
- Create a fresh database backup.
- Export user configs if controlled vocabularies, record-export presets,
  document templates, print-layout presets, or custom fields are needed to
  reproduce the work.
- Create a full project export with `Light export` off so media are included,
  checking record counts and missing-media warnings first.
- Add tabular record exports or a data package if the data will be analyzed,
  published, or reused outside NAHPU.

Name files clearly with the project, export type, and date, such as
`BorneoMammals_project_20241015.tar.gz`. Store the project export, database
backup, user-config file, and a short readme together.

Keep at least two copies in separate locations, and retain the original until
the archive opens cleanly on another device. Record the archive date, contents,
storage locations, and any access restrictions, and periodically test-open or
test-restore the files. Follow your institution's retention policies for both
digital files and printed documents.

## Before sharing data {% #before-sharing-data %}

Before sending NAHPU data to a museum, collaborator, or another device:

- Create a fresh database backup.
- Choose the right project export:
  - `Light export` (`JSON.GZ`) when the recipient needs records without media
    (such as over a slow connection);
  - `TAR.GZ` or `ZIP` when media or a complete copy must travel with the
    project.
- Export user configs if the recipient needs your controlled vocabularies,
  presets, templates, layouts, or custom fields.
- Include any tabular exports, bundles, or documents the recipient requested.
- Verify that at least one restore path works before deleting anything from the
  source device.
